# Python Port Plan — Step 05 · The Agent Loop

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/05_agent_loop` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/05_agent_loop` that is behaviourally equivalent:
same `Boukensha::Agent#run` loop (call → normalize response → dispatch
tool calls or return final text → repeat, with an iteration ceiling and a
tools-disabled wind-down call), same normalized `{stop_reason, content}`
shape every backend produces via `parse_response`, same round-trip
`assistant_message`/`assistant_parts` rebuilding on the next request.

This is the sixth and final entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>`
series for `week1_baseline` (see [`context.md`](context.md) for the
index). `04_api_client` is the direct predecessor (not yet ported at the
time of this writing — see [`04_api_client`](04_api_client)); this step
adds `Boukensha::Agent` and extends every backend + `PromptBuilder` +
`Client` with response-parsing/replay support. This is where BOUKENSHA
becomes an actual tool-calling agent rather than a single request/response
demo.

Confirmed via `diff` against `04_api_client`: `registry.rb`, `tool.rb`,
`message.rb`, `tasks/player.rb`, `Gemfile`/`.lock` are byte-identical.
`config.rb` only changes cosmetically (endless-method syntax for
`mud_host`/`mud_port`/etc. — no behavioural change, and Python's `config.py`
already uses `@property` so there's nothing to port). Everything else
listed below has a real, meaningful diff.

